  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 139 PM CDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\nA weak upper level trough of low pressure currently over western ND \nstill may become the focal point for showers and storms impacting \nthe western CWA later this evening. A few showers and storms are \nongoing, with some model guidance suggesting modest intensity over \nthe next few hours. However, the best low level moisture and \ninstability is located mainly west of the CWA, with SBCAPE exceeding \n1K J/kg over western SD. Across much of eastern ND, and northeastern \nSD, surface dewpoints in the 50s with limited moisture advection at \nthis time. As of now, the RRFS is the more robust model solution \nwith convection developing over the western Dakotas. The storms in \nwestern SD move eastward toward the CWA around 23-0Z before \ndiminishing. It still maintains a convection complex over southwest \nND moving southward into north central SD after 1Z. The lack of a \nLLJ may limit the overall severe threat with the RRFS showing the \nstorms weakening with time. The past few runs of the HRRR keeps most \nof the CWA free from severe weather. Overall, the severe threat is \nbest west of the Missouri River where a few storms may produce golf \nball size hail and 60-75 mph wind gusts. The pcpn should exit the \nCWA before 12Z Monday.\n\nA frontal boundary will cross the region Monday through Monday night \nmay produce an isolated storm during the day period. Models due \nindicate convection developing after 0Z Tuesday, with eastern ND \nhaving a good potential for storms. A lee of the Black Hills surface \nlow may cause additional storms over central and south central SD. \nWhile widespread severe weather is not anticipated, a stronger storm \nor two could produce golf ball size hail and wind gusts of 60 to 75 \nmph.\n\nAfter dry conditions midweek, chances for precipitation returns to \nthe forecast area Thursday night through the weekend. Temperatures \nremain at or above average for this time of year.",
